#{[17592186476951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476951 #edb/ref 1071 "The linear transformation $T$ has the standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat are the respective images of the standard basis vectors $\\{i,j\\}$ of ${R}^{2}$ under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9c1cac7-603f-5a47-87fd-f2ee952aaad9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476951 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ are the first and second columns of $T,$ respectively. \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot i$ and $T\\cdot j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\cdot i=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T\\cdot j=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479400 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479400 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479400 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "194e6537-4699-5a25-81a6-e3f4bb381d20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479406 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921864